WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:03.180
If you’ve ever been stung by a jellyfish,

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.600
you know that their sting can pack a mean punch.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.780
That stinger is actually a tiny missile

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.220
launched from a cell on the jelly’s tentacle

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.100
Both microbes and cnidarians

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:14.300
such as jellyfish,

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.840
wield these harpoon-like weapons

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.500
called nematocysts.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.420
Microbes like dinoflagellates,

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.940
have evolved their own arsenal

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:23.640
of these tiny weapons.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.600
This is polykrikos kofoidii

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.520
hunting for dinner.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.180
We’ve known for almost 100 years

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:30.320
that these miniscule creatures

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.440
were hunting and fighting with weapons

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:33.940
but thanks to new technology

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.940
we can now observe them in stunning detail.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.920
Using focused ion beam scanning microscopy,

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.780
researchers imaged and modeled

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.860
two different dinoflagellates.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.140
They found that their nematocysts

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.660
are surprisingly sophisticated when compared

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.920
to their animal counterparts.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.380
Even though jellyfish nematocysts

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.600
are 20 times larger,

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.600
they have far fewer moving parts.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:57.920
In polykrikos kofoidii,

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:58.960
each nematocyst

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.260
is made up of a capsule

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.480
with a stylet nestled inside.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.680
When they sense their prey is within reach,

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:05.920
they fire off the stylet,

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.460
bursting through the operculum,

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.040
and literally tow their catch in to eat.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.920
Then the tow line dissolves.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.960
Researchers also studied another type of dinoflagellate

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.820
that employed a “gatling gun”,

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.560
with 10-15 stylets arranged in a circle.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.460
Despite the similarity to nematocysts

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.460
found in jellyfish and its relatives,

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.400
one is not derived from the other--

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.340
they evolved independently.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.860
In fact, the ancestors of dinoflagellates

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.800
were photosynthetic, relying on the power

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.090
of the sun to create energy.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.820
Over time, that they lost their ability to

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.400
harness the sun’s energy

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.520
and became full time hunters.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.100
Now, they’ve spent millennia

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.180
honing their
weapons,

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:45.880
gearing up their gatling guns, and

